<?xml version="1.0"?>
<Definitions xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <CubeBlocks>

    <!--
      Ground Truth - Environmental Instruments

      Models are MTGraves' Naval Theme Prop Pack (2494017078), used with his explicit
      permission and credited in ATTRIBUTION.txt, the Workshop description and beside
      each Model line below.

      The general purpose antenna at the bottom of this file is a RadioAntenna and is
      NOT an instrument - it carries no properties and broadcast is its whole purpose.

      WHY UpgradeModule

      Instruments need two things from their base type: a NARROW interface, and a
      detail info pane to write readings into. Getting here took three attempts and one
      wrong conclusion, all of which are worth recording.

      FunctionalBlockDefinition was first. Behaviourally right - a generic powered
      terminal block with nothing intrinsic attached - and wrong on the interface.
      Terminal properties register against an INTERFACE, and FunctionalBlock means
      IMyFunctionalBlock, which every powered block in the game implements. The 55 GT_
      properties landed on every light, door and thruster in the world and corrupted
      their terminal control lists: a light lost its On/Off action, in every world this
      mod loaded into. Confirmed by bisect 2026-08-09. Working mods all register
      against a narrow type they own - Nanobot Build and Repair puts 27 properties on
      IMyShipWelder and is fine. Count was never the problem; breadth was.

      OreDetector was second, and was rejected on a finding that turned out to be
      FALSE. The claim was "OreDetector renders no detail info panel at all". It does
      render one. What actually happened is that the test never called
      RefreshCustomInfo, so the game never asked our writer for text and the pane was
      empty rather than absent. Proved by probes/PaneProbe 2026-08-10, where an
      OreDetector clone shows the text perfectly. A wrong conclusion, held for a day,
      that sent the whole design down the third path.

      RadioAntenna was third, and shipped for a day. It renders a pane and its
      interface is narrow, and MaxBroadcastRadius 1 made the radio side inert. What it
      could not do is stop drawing on the HUD. Every working antenna puts a marker on
      its owner's HUD, four instruments put four markers in your face, and the flag
      behind it - MyRadioBroadcaster.ShowOnHud - is computed from IsWorking, is
      get-only, and sits on a type the mod whitelist prohibits. Verified on a VANILLA
      antenna with every terminal box unchecked. Removing the broadcaster component
      does suppress it and then crashes the game when the terminal opens. There is no
      route to a marker-free antenna from mod code.

      UpgradeModule is where this lands. IMyUpgradeModule extends IMyFunctionalBlock
      with two members, so it is narrower than the antenna was. It renders the pane. It
      has no broadcaster, so no HUD marker. It is not a lightning rod, which the
      antenna quietly was. With no <Upgrades> element it does nothing at all when
      placed beside a refinery.

      The one thing it does not inherit is a power draw - MyObjectBuilder_
      UpgradeModuleDefinition has exactly one field, Upgrades, and no ResourceSinkGroup.
      Any power cost these blocks have must be attached in code.


      "Alt" variants are the same instrument in a different silhouette - same role, same
      capabilities, same code path. They exist so a builder can pick a shape, not because
      they measure differently, and nothing in the readouts implies otherwise.

      Large and small grid entries share a BlockPairName so the size toggle works.
    -->

    <Definition xsi:type="MyObjectBuilder_UpgradeModuleDefinition">
      <Id>
        <TypeId>UpgradeModule</TypeId>
        <SubtypeId>GT_RadiationMonitor</SubtypeId>
      </Id>
      <DisplayName>Radiation Monitor</DisplayName>
      <Description>Measures ionising exposure at this position and reports survivable time. Threshold Dynamics, Survey and Assessment.</Description>
      <Icon>Textures\Icons\MED_Radar_Dome_Icon.dds</Icon>
      <CubeSize>Large</CubeSize>
      <BlockTopology>TriangleMesh</BlockTopology>
      <Size x="1" y="1" z="1" />
      <Center x="0" y="0" z="0" />
      <ModelOffset x="0" y="0" z="0" />
      <!-- Model by MTGraves, Naval Theme Prop Pack (2494017078), used with permission. -->
      <Model>Models\Cubes\Large\LG_1x1x1_Radar_Dome.mwm</Model>
      <Components>
        <Component Subtype="SteelPlate" Count="20" />
        <Component Subtype="Construction" Count="15" />
        <Component Subtype="SmallTube" Count="15" />
        <Component Subtype="Detector" Count="10" />
        <Component Subtype="Computer" Count="15" />
        <Component Subtype="SteelPlate" Count="10" />
      </Components>
      <CriticalComponent Subtype="Detector" Index="0" />
      <MountPoints>

      </MountPoints>
      <BuildProgressModels>
        <Model BuildPercentUpperBound="0.30" File="Models\Cubes\Large\LG_1x1x1_Radar_Dome_BS1.mwm" />
        <Model BuildPercentUpperBound="0.70" File="Models\Cubes\Large\LG_1x1x1_Radar_Dome_BS2.mwm" />
        <Model BuildPercentUpperBound="1.00" File="Models\Cubes\Large\LG_1x1x1_Radar_Dome_BS3.mwm" />
      </BuildProgressModels>
      <BlockPairName>GT_RadiationMonitor</BlockPairName>
      <MirroringY>Z</MirroringY>
      <MirroringZ>Y</MirroringZ>
      <EdgeType>Light</EdgeType>
      <BuildTimeSeconds>20</BuildTimeSeconds>
      <DamageEffectName>Damage_Electrical_Damaged</DamageEffectName>
      <DamagedSound>ParticleElectrical</DamagedSound>
      <EmissiveColorPreset>Default</EmissiveColorPreset>
      <DestroyEffect>BlockDestroyedExplosion_Large</DestroyEffect>
      <DestroySound>WepSmallWarheadExpl</DestroySound>
      <PCU>25</PCU>
      <IsAirTight>false</IsAirTight>
    </Definition>

